Most agentic reverse-engineering demos I've seen share an unspoken assumption: a frontier model is on the other end of the wire. The decompilation gets pasted in whole. The model reasons. Everyone nods.

That demo breaks the moment the model is a 30B-class quant running on your own inference server. The context window is smaller, and every token in it is paid for on every later turn. Tool-call reliability drops. Long procedural instructions drift. The model loops when it's stuck instead of stopping.

You can respond to that by waiting for better models. Or you can treat it as a design constraint and build around it. This post is about the second path: a personal agentic RE stack, two applications (PARE and PAL) on a shared runtime (`agent_core`), that holds up *because* of the constraints, not despite them.

The claim is not "a local model matches a frontier model." It's that **architecture can substitute where raw capability falls short**, and that the substitutes are worth building even if you do have a frontier key.

## The shape of the system

Three layers:

- **`agent_core`** — the shared runtime: daemon lifecycle, OpenAI-compatible inference clients, retrieval, tool and command registries, and the worker layer.
- **[PARE](https://github.com/EdibleTuber/PARE)** (Personal Agentic Reverse Engineer) — a conversational mobile-RE operator that drives workers: static APK analysis, Frida instrumentation, HTTPS interception, and a UART console out on a physical bench.
- **[PAL](https://github.com/EdibleTuber/PAL)** (Personal Agentic Librarian) — the other side of the coin: a knowledge manager that curates an Obsidian-style vault, which PARE consults by semantic search when it needs curated knowledge rather than whatever its weights happen to contain.

Everything runs on hardware in my own house. A local OpenAI-compatible inference server (Ollama, llama.cpp, vLLM all work). No model API calls leave the LAN.

The producer/consumer split between PAL and PARE is the part most personal-memory designs collapse into one agent with a database table. It matters: knowledge is *authored* under one set of consent rules and *consumed* under another, and neither agent has to be good at the other's job.

## PARE: methodology as software

A tool-using agent improvises. An RE investigator follows a method. PARE's central bet is that the method can be encoded, and that it has to be encoded precisely because the model won't invent it on its own.

The loop:

1. **Orient** — from the behaviour the operator describes, find the region of code or traffic that could produce it. The operator's description is a lead to corroborate, not ground truth.
2. **Enumerate** — build the *candidate set* before committing: every site that could cause the symptom, not the first plausible idiom.
3. **Hypothesize** — pick one candidate and pin what should be observable at runtime.
4. **Verify** — confirm it against a live capture. An empty capture means "not triggered yet." A *contradicting* value means the target was wrong.
5. **Re-orient** — on a dead end, advance to the next candidate; on a surprise, go back to static analysis to explain it.

Prose in a system prompt won't hold a model to this by itself. Two guards enforce it mechanically:

- A **repeat-guard**: a tool call that repeats with the same result is short-circuited instead of spinning to the round cap. Local models loop more; the floor catches it.
- **Operator-handback checkpoints**: the agent ends its turn with a question when it's stuck, or when it's about to commit to one of several near-duplicate candidates. A silent wrong commit becomes a one-line correction. This is the discipline a 70B+ model would half-impose on itself, made structural.

## The capture store: context you only pay for twice

RE tools produce enormous output: an hour of HTTPS flows, every class in an APK, a module's exports. The naive agent pastes it all into the conversation and burns its window in a few calls.

PARE stores every worker result in a capture store and lets only small results through to the model. Large results are replaced by a stub of at most 512 bytes: what was captured, how many rows, which columns, and a `ref`. The model then *asks for just what it needs* — searching everything with `search_capture` and reading one result at a time with `read_capture`.

Two properties fall out of this that I didn't fully anticipate:

- **Findings outlive the call that produced them.** A capture from three days ago is still searchable; the investigation accumulates instead of evaporating with the context window.
- **Nothing is thrown away.** The operator can view any capture in full, straight from the store, with no model in the path. The human reads the ground truth; the model reads the stubs of ground truth it needs.

This is the design pattern that made me confident about adding new workers: any tool whose output can be shaped into *rows with columns* plugs into PARE's loop without changing the conversation model at all.

## Workers, risk tiers, and the bench

Workers are registered with risk tiers. Passive and read-tier tools execute; anything that changes target or device state pauses for operator approval, and every call is audited. That single mechanism resolves the tension that makes most open-source security agents unusable: they're either fully autonomous (reckless with a live target) or fully manual (a chatbot with extra steps).

The hardware tier runs on a Raspberry Pi at the workbench. The first worker there, [pare-hardware-mcp](https://github.com/EdibleTuber/pare-hardware-mcp), exposes a UART console session from a Tigard debugger to agents: device listing, baud detection, open/close, reading captured output, sending commands. Its internal contract is hardware-free — the tool surface was TDD'd against canned artifacts before it ever touched a board, which is a pattern worth more than any single tool in it.

## PAL: consent tiers instead of a write-protect switch

PAL manages the vault, and the interesting decisions are all about *when the agent may act*:

- **Vault reads** flow freely.
- **Vault writes** are autonomous, but every write is git-committed automatically — an agent that can act freely, and a history you can always review or revert.
- **Web fetches and wiki promotions** are consent-gated: PAL proposes and waits.

That gradient — read everything, write recoverably, commit irreversibly only with a human — is a more correct shape than both common alternatives: gate everything (useless) or gate nothing (a prompt injection away from disaster).

PAL's security posture is treated as a feature and documented as one: prompt-injection sanitization on fetched content, vault-scoped path checks on every file operation, a domain allowlist on fetches, Unix socket ACLs, and Discord allowlisting with per-channel context isolation.

## Why run local at all

Honest accounting:

**What you lose.** Raw capability. Protocol inference from hexdumps, long-series reasoning, and tool-call reliability all land lower on a 30B-class quant than on a frontier API. Expect to verify hypotheses a frontier agent would nail unassisted.

**What you get.** A boundary. For an agent whose job is examining hostile software and untrusted network traffic, the fact that no model API ever sees the target data is not a compromise, it's a threat-model requirement. Add: inference you can audit end to end, no per-token metering during long investigations, and no vendor deciding to deprecate your model mid-investigation.

**What makes it work at all** is the architecture: stubs instead of pasted dumps, guards instead of good judgment, consent tiers instead of unlimited autonomy. Each of those substitutes is *also* what a well-run frontier deployment looks like. Building them against a small model just made it non-optional.

## What's next: signals

The next worker points the loop at the RF spectrum. A Linux handheld with sub-GHz and LoRa isn't just a toy, it's a programmable radio data source. The same bench topology applies — a worker exposing set-frequency / capture / read / transmit tools, transmit behind the risk gate, every capture landing in the capture store as rows the model can search and read by window.

The five-beat loop transfers almost unchanged. Only the Verify instrument differs: instead of a Frida hook, the runtime check is a replay.

